.TH RC 1
.SH NAME
rc, cd, eval, exec, exit, flag, rfork, shift, wait, whatis, builtin, ., ~ \- command language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rc
[
.B \-eixslv
]
[
.B \-c
.I command
]
[
.I file
[
.I arg ...
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rc
is Vectra's shell, Plan 9's grammar carried over whole.
It reads command lines from the string after
.BR \-c ,
from
.IR file ,
or from descriptor 0, in that order of preference, and runs each as it
is read.
With no
.B \-c
and no
.IR file ,
descriptor 0 is read interactively:
.B $prompt
.RI ( 1 )
is written before a command and
.B $prompt
.RI ( 2 )
before a line that continues it, and a syntax error costs the line, not
the session.
.SS Command Lines
A command line is a sequence of commands separated by ampersands or
semicolons
.RB ( &
or
.BR ; ),
terminated by a newline, and run in sequence left to right.
.I Rc
does not wait for a command followed by
.B &
before starting the next one.
Whenever such a command starts, its process id is put in
.BR $apid .
Whenever a command not followed by
.B &
ends, its wait word is put in
.BR $status ,
empty for success.
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR rc ,
a trailing backslash does not continue a line onto the next;
a line continues only inside an unclosed
.B {
or
.BR ( ,
or after
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
.B |
or the closing
.B )
of
.BR if ,
.B for
or
.BR while .
A number sign
.RB ( # )
and everything up to the next newline are ignored, except inside quotes.
.SS Simple Commands
A simple command is a sequence of arguments interspersed with I/O
redirections.
If the first argument is the name of a function or one of
.IR rc 's
builtins, it is run by
.I rc
itself.
Otherwise, if the name starts with
.BR / ,
it is the path of the program to run.
A name with no
.B /
is searched for along the directories named in
.BR $path ;
the first name found is exec'd, and a name that exists but will not
exec ends the search with its own error rather than trying the rest of
the path.
The first word of a simple command cannot be a keyword unless it is
quoted or otherwise disguised.
The keywords are
.EX
	for in while if not switch fn ~ ! @
.EE
.SS Arguments
Most constructions that stand for an argument are worth a list of
strings, not a single one.
The simplest is the unquoted word: a run of characters none of which is
a blank, a tab, a newline, or one of
.EX
	# ; & | ^ $ ` ' { } ( ) < >
.EE
An unquoted word holding
.BR * ,
.B ?
or
.B [
is a pattern (see Patterns, below).
A quoted word is a run of characters between single quotes
.RB ( ' );
a quote inside one is written twice
.RB ( '' ).
Each of the following is an argument.
.TP
.BI ( arguments )
The value is a list made of every argument in the parenthesized sequence.
.TP
.BI $ argument
.TP
.BI $ argument ( subscript )
The variable named by
.I argument
is substituted, an alias for
.RI $* ( argument )
when
.I argument
is a number.
A subscript picks elements by one-based index, singly
.RI ( "$x(1)" ),
several at once
.RI ( "$x(3 1)" ),
or as a range
.RI ( "$x(2-4)" ", " "$x(2-)" ", " "$x(-3)" ).
An index off the end of the list contributes nothing.
.TP
.BI $# argument
The value is the count of elements in the named variable, as one string.
.TP
.BI $\(dq argument
The value is the named variable's elements joined by one space, as one
string.
.TP
.BI ` "{ " command " }"
.I Rc
runs
.I command
and splits its standard output on the characters in
.BR $ifs ,
dropping empty pieces.
The pid it waited for is left in
.BR $bqstatus .
.TP
.IB argument ^ argument
The two operands are concatenated.
Equal-length lists pair off; a list of one against a longer list is
distributed across it; any other mismatch is an error.
.PP
Pattern matching, where it applies, is done after every other
operation, so
.B x=/tmp echo $x^/*.c
matches
.BR /tmp/*.c ,
not
.B /*.c
prefixed afterwards.
.SS Free Carets
.I Rc
inserts the
.B ^
operator between two arguments that touch with no space between them.
Whenever
.BR $ ,
.B '
or
.B `
follows a quoted or unquoted word, or an unquoted word follows a quoted
one, with nothing between, a caret is inserted.
If an unquoted word immediately follows
.B $
and holds a character that is not a letter, a digit, an underscore or
.BR * ,
a caret is inserted before that character, so
.B /proc/$pid/status
is the variable
.B $pid
and the word
.B /status
joined by a free caret, not an unset variable named
.BR pid/status .
.SS I/O Redirections
.BI > file
redirects standard output (descriptor 1) to
.IR file ;
.BI >> file
appends.
.BI < file
redirects standard input (descriptor 0);
.BI <> file
opens
.I file
once, for reading and writing.
.BI << tag
reads a here document: the lines up to one that is exactly
.I tag
become standard input, with
.BI $ name
substituted unless
.I tag
was quoted, and
.B $$
standing for one
.BR $ .
A redirection may be qualified with a descriptor number in brackets,
.BR "cc x.c >[2]log" ,
and one descriptor may be duplicated onto another,
.BR >[ fd0 = fd1 ],
.BR <[ fd0 = fd1 ]
or
.BR <>[ fd0 = fd1 ],
or closed,
.BR >[ fd0 =]
or
.BR <[ fd0 =] .
Redirections take effect left to right, so
.B cc x.c >/dev/null >[2=1]
and
.B cc x.c >[2=1] >/dev/null
differ.
A command that runs in a child keeps its redirections; one that runs in
.I rc
itself has them undone once it finishes.
.SS Pipelines
A pair of commands joined by
.B |
is a pipeline: the left's standard output feeds the right's standard
input.
.BI |[ fd ]
connects the pipe to descriptor
.I fd
of the left command instead of 1;
.BI |[ fd0 = fd1 ]
connects
.I fd1
of the left to
.I fd0
of the right.
The status of a pipeline is its stages' statuses joined by
.BR | .
.SS Compound Commands
.PD 0
.TP
.BI "if( " list " ) " command
Runs
.I command
if
.I list
left
.B $status
empty.
.TP
.BI "if not " command
Runs
.I command
if the
.I list
of the
.B if
immediately before it did not.
.TP
.BI "for( " name " in " arguments " ) " command
.TP
.BI "for( " name " ) " command
Runs
.I command
once per argument, with
.I name
bound to it.
With no
.I arguments
list,
.B $*
is used.
.TP
.BI "while( " list " ) " command
Runs
.I command
each time
.I list
leaves
.B $status
empty; an empty
.I list
counts as empty status.
.TP
.BI "switch( " argument "){" cases "}"
Matches
.I argument
against the patterns of each top-level
.B case
command in
.I cases
and runs the commands up to the next
.B case
or the closing brace.
.TP
.BI { list }
Groups a sequence of commands, changing what an operator around it binds.
.TP
.BI "fn " name " {" list "}"
.TP
.BI "fn " name
Defines
.I name
as a function running
.IR list ,
with the remainder of the command's own argument list bound to
.B $*
for the duration.
With no body, removes the definition.
.TP
.BI "~ " subject " " patterns ...
Sets
.B $status
empty if
.I subject
matches any
.IR pattern ,
and to
.B "no match"
otherwise.
.TP
.BI ! " command"
Inverts
.BR $status :
empty becomes
.BR false ,
non-empty becomes empty.
.TP
.BI "@ " command
Runs
.I command
in a subshell: a forked
.I rc
that ends when it does, and whose status becomes this one's.
.TP
.IB name = "argument [command]"
Sets
.I name
to
.I argument
for the duration of
.IR command ,
restoring the old value after, or for good if
.I command
is omitted.
.PD
.SS Variables
Values are lists of strings.
.I Rc
keeps a directory entry in
.B /env
for every variable whose value is non-empty (see
.IR env (3)),
so a program it starts sees what it set; an emptied variable's file is
removed.
.BR $* ,
.BR $0 ,
.BR $status ,
.B $apid
and
.B $bqstatus
are
.IR rc 's
own and are never written to
.BR /env .
Vectra's
.I rc
does not yet write a function's body to
.B /env
as Plan 9's
.B fn#name
does, so a child
.I rc
does not inherit its parent's functions.
.SS Patterns
An unquoted
.BR * ,
.B ?
or
.B [
marks a word as a pattern.
.B *
matches any run of characters,
.B ?
matches one, and
.BI [ class ]
matches one character in
.IR class ,
which is negated if its first character is
.B ~
and may hold ranges written
.IB lo - hi .
Where a pattern names a file, it is matched a path component at a time
against a directory listing, sorted, skipping names that begin with
.B .
unless the pattern's component does too;
.B /
must appear in the pattern itself.
A pattern that matches nothing is not replaced by nothing: it stands
for itself, so
.B rm *.tmp
with no
.B .tmp
file says
.B "no such file"
rather than removing nothing silently.
Where a pattern is matched against a string instead of a file, as in
.B ~
and
.BR case ,
there is no exception for a leading
.BR . .
.SS Builtins
These run inside
.I rc
itself because they change its own state.
.TP
.BI "." " [\-i] file ..."
Runs commands from
.IR file ,
with
.B $*
set to the arguments after it for the duration.
.B \-i
makes the run interactive: prompted, and a syntax error does not end it.
As a special case,
.B /dev/cons
and
.B /fd/0
name descriptor 0 itself.
.TP
.BI "builtin " command " ..."
Runs
.I command
as a builtin even where a function of the same name exists.
.TP
.BI "cd [" dir "]"
Changes directory to
.IR dir ,
default
.BR $home .
There is no
.B $cdpath
search.
.TP
.BI "eval [" "arg ..." "]"
Joins the arguments with spaces and runs the result as input.
.TP
.BI "exec [" "command ..." "]"
Replaces
.I rc
with
.IR command ,
its redirections kept rather than undone.
With no
.IR command ,
only the redirections take effect, and stay.
.TP
.BI "exit [" status "]"
Ends
.I rc
with
.IR status ,
default the current
.BR $status .
.TP
.BI "flag " f " [+\-]"
Sets
.RB ( + ),
clears
.RB ( \- )
or tests (neither) the single-character flag
.IR f ,
one of the command line flags below or any letter a script wants for
its own use.
.TP
.BR "rfork " "[nNeEsfFm]"
Becomes a new process group with
.IR fork (2)'s
.B rfork
flags by letter:
.B n
.RB ( RFNAMEG ),
.B N
.RB ( RFCNAMEG ),
.B e
.RB ( RFENVG ),
.B E
.RB ( RFCENVG ),
.B s
.RB ( RFNOTEG ),
.B f
.RB ( RFFDG ),
.B F
.RB ( RFCFDG ),
.B m
.RB ( RFNOMNT ).
With no letters, the default is
.BR ens .
.TP
.BI "shift [" n "]"
Removes the first
.I n
(default 1) elements of
.BR $* .
.TP
.BI "wait [" pid "]"
Waits for
.IR pid ,
or for every outstanding child when none is given, leaving the last
one's word in
.BR $status .
.TP
.BI whatis " name ..."
Prints what
.I name
is: an assignment if it is a variable,
.B "fn name {...}"
if it is a function, a
.B builtin
line if it is a builtin, or the path
.B $path
would find.
.PD
.SS Notes
A note not caught ends
.I rc
the way it ends any program, with the note as its status.
.B interrupt
is the exception: it is continued from, so a prompt or a read it cut
short is asked for again, and the command it reached is what ends.
There is no way, yet, to bind a function to a note by name, as Plan 9's
.BI "fn " note
does.
.SS Environment
.I Rc
reads
.B /lib/rcmain
before its first command, which gives
.BR $path ,
.BR $home ,
.B $ifs
and
.B $prompt
their defaults, each only where
.B /env
did not already hold one:
.B $path
becomes
.RB ( ". /bin" ),
.B $home
becomes
.BR /usr/glenda ,
.B $ifs
becomes a space, a tab and a newline, and
.B $prompt
becomes
.RB ( "'% ' '\et'" ).
.SH SOURCE
.B apps/rc
.br
.B apps/rc/rcmain
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (1),
.IR bind (1),
.IR env (1),
.IR fork (2),
.IR wait (2),
.IR notify (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B $status
is empty on success and a reason otherwise.
A syntax error sets it to
.BR "syntax error" .
.B \-e
makes a non-interactive
.I rc
stop at the first simple command whose status is non-empty.
.B \-x
writes each simple command to descriptor 2 before it runs.
.BR \-s ,
.B \-l
and
.B \-v
are accepted but presently have no effect.
.SH BUGS
There is no
.BI <{ command }
or
.BI >{ command }
form, which need
.B /fd
to name a pipe as a file.
Backquote always splits on
.BR $ifs ;
there is no form that names its own separators.
.I Whatis
prints
.B "fn name {...}"
rather than the function's actual body.
